Ch:\nraft.go:873:\t\tcase future := <-r.userRestoreCh:\nraft.go:880:\t\t\terr := r.restoreUserSnapshot(future.meta, future.reader)\nraft.go:1097:// restoreUserSnapshot is used to manually consume an external snapshot, such\nraft.go:1105:func (r *Raft) restoreUserSnapshot(meta *SnapshotMeta, reader io.Reader) error {\nraft.go:1106:\tdefer metrics.MeasureSince([]string{\"raft\", \"restoreUserSnapshot\"}, time.Now())\napi.go:180:\t// userRestoreCh is used for user-triggered restores of external\napi.go:182:\tuserRestoreCh chan *userRestoreFuture\napi.go:572:\t\tuserRestoreCh:         make(chan *userRestoreFuture),\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nold=json.load(open('update.json'))\nti=old['interpretations'][1]  # transfer account from last turn\n\nti['executions']={\\\"23acf06d8a524b2790ef674d5eb04cb0\\\":[0,1,2,3]}\nti['conclusion']=\\\"explained\\\"\nti['defect_claim']=\\\"asserted\\\"\nti['answers']={\\\"627b3ef0c6e049bfb836e40ffbdce875-gap-1\\\":{\n \\\"explanation\\\":(\\\"Resolved by the revised probe (receipt 23acf06d8a524b2790ef674d5eb04cb0). In the non-voter case (result event index 3) the previous leader was outside Leader state in every one of the 1289 samples taken from the instant of the call, and at least one sample found no leader in the cluster: 241 of those samples were leaderless, the first at 1ms and the last at 278ms after the call, so with a 200ms election timeout the cluster was observed without a leader for about 277ms, roughly 1.4 election timeouts, while the requested target never held leadership and the maximum term rose from 2 to 4 with a different voter holding leadership at the end. The control case with a voter target (result event index 1) recorded no leaderless sample at all; its sampling window was about one millisecond because the transfer completed immediately, so its zero count is a short window rather than evidence about longer gaps.\\\"),\n \\\"resolved\\\":True}}\n\nti['rationale']=(\\\"The question was whether LeadershipTransferToServer reports a transfer that the named server can complete when that server is a non-voter, and what happens to leadership while it cannot; both halves are now execution-backed. Naming a voter (receipt 23acf06d8a524b2790ef674d5eb04cb0, setup index 0 and result index 1, and the earlier receipt b2c0026c56d54511999e5c37ef8372cd) the call returned nil, the requested server was Leader at the endpoint and the previous leader Follower, so the call does perform real transfers. Naming the non-voter (setup index 2, result index 3) the same call returned nil while the requested server was never observed as Leader and finished Follower; the previous leader was outside Leader state in all 1289 samples taken from the instant of the call; 241 samples found no leader in the cluster at all, the first 1ms and the last 278ms after the call, so the cluster was leaderless for about 277ms against a 200ms election timeout; the maximum term rose from 2 to 4; and leadership ended on a voter other than the requested target. \\\"\n \\\"The source explains the mechanism as predicted. The leader resolves the requested ID only through leaderState.replState, which startStopReplication populates for every configured server except the leader without consulting Suffrage, so a non-voter passes and receives TimeoutNow; timeoutNow sets it to Candidate and marks candidateFromLeadershipTransfer, a flag that by design suppresses pre-vote, so it sends a real RequestVote carrying the next term. Every voter adopts the higher term and becomes a follower before reaching the hasVote check, which then refuses the vote because the candidate holds no vote, so the requested server can never collect a grant. The old leader learns of the higher term, steps down, and its transfer goroutine reports success from the leftLeaderLoop branch, so the caller is told the handover succeeded while leadership has left it for a server it did not choose. \\\"\n \\\"That mismatch is the defect: the documented purpose of the call is to hand leadership to the named server, and a success result that the named server can never satisfy leaves the caller unable to distinguish a completed handover from a self-inflicted interruption that costs the cluster the leaderless interval it takes to elect someone else. The interruption is bounded rather than unbounded, and its length is now measured rather than inferred. No data loss is claimed: committed entries stay committed, and the control case shows the probe does observe a completed transfer.\\\")\n\nti['limitations']=[\n \\\"The reported-result and interval observations rest on two executions of this probe (receipts b2c0026c56d54511999e5c37ef8372cd and 23acf06d8a524b2790ef674d5eb04cb0); across them the leader holding office at the end differed (in the first, the previous leader was Leader again at term 4; in the second, another voter), so the identity of the successor is timing dependent and is recorded rather than asserted.\\\",\n \\\"The leaderless interval is an observed span at 1ms sampling resolution between a 200ms election timeout and a single-process in-memory transport; it is not a bound for configurations with different timeouts, and it does not distinguish time spent campaigning from time waiting for election timers.\\\",\n \\\"The control case with a voter target finished in about one millisecond, so its sampling window is too short for its zero leaderless count to say anything about longer gaps.\\\",\n \\\"The claim concerns the reported result of the call, the requested target's inability to win and the observed interruption; it does not assert data loss or that the cluster fails to recover, since a voter is elected afterwards.\\\",\n \\\"The probe uses one process with real Raft instances and in-memory transports, so it exercises the leader's own transfer, election and vote paths but not process-level partitions or multi-host clocks.\\\"]\n\nknowledge={\n \\\"ref\\\":\\\"@transfer_nonvoter_disruption\\\",\n \\\"kind\\\":\\\"fact\\\",\n \\\"statement\\\":\\\"A leadership transfer requested for a non-voter leaves the cluster without a leader until a voter wins the next election, while the call itself has already reported success. In the observed run with HeartbeatTimeout = ElectionTimeout = LeaderLeaseTimeout = 200ms, the call returned nil, the requested non-voter was never Leader, the previous leader was sampled outside Leader state in all 1289 samples from the instant of the call, 241 samples found no leader at all (first 1ms, last 278ms after the call, about 1.4 election timeouts), the maximum term rose from 2 to 4, and a different voter held leadership at the end. The voter-target control with the same call returned nil and did hand leadership to the requested server.\\\",\n \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Cluster of three voters plus one non-voter joined through AddNonvoter, protocol version >= 3, all peers reachable, pre-vote left at its default, and a single process with in-memory transports; the target is chosen by its suffrage in the leader's latest configuration, not by any observed result.\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"api_transfer_to_server\\\",\\\"transfer_target_lookup\\\",\\\"timeout_now\\\",\\\"candidate_skips_prevote\\\",\\\"vote_term_bump\\\",\\\"transfer_lost_leadership\\\",\\\"pick_server_voters\\\",\\\"start_stop_repl\\\"],\n \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"23acf06d8a524b2790ef674d5eb04cb0\\\":[0,1,2,3],\\\"b2c0026c56d54511999e5c37ef8372cd\\\":[0,1,2,3]}}\n\nd={\\\"rationale\\\":(\\\"Interpreted the revised transfer probe: all six comparisons hold, and the liveness half of the question is now measured rather than inferred. In the non-voter case the previous leader was outside Leader state in every one of the 1289 samples taken from the instant of the call, 241 samples found no leader at all (first 1ms, last 278ms after the call, about 1.4 election timeouts against a 200ms election timeout), the maximum term rose from 2 to 4, and leadership ended on a voter other than the requested target, while the call had already returned nil; the voter-target control recorded a completed transfer. The gap about the interruption is therefore resolved, and the measured disruption is promoted as a Fact because it is durable and will be reused. The question account stays scoped: it asserts the wrong success report and the observed interruption, not data loss or failure to recover.\\\"),\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[knowledge],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[ti]}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False); open('update.json','a').write('\\\\n')\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
